# sssd-u2f (Custom Authselect Profile)

This is a custom **authselect** profile based on `sssd` that provides a
"Context-Aware" U2F authentication flow. It balances strong security for
graphical login with frictionless convenience for administrative tasks.

Tested with: Fedora 44 and YubiKey Bio - FIDO Edition (early edition), YubiKey 5C NFC,
YubiKey 5C Nano. YubiKey 4 is not supported (no FIDO2).

The FIDO2 key files are shared with the SSH client — the same `~/.ssh/u2f_*`
files are used for both system login/sudo and SSH authentication.

---

## Key Features

Unlike the standard Fedora/RHEL U2F setup, this profile uses **paired
guard blocks** in the PAM stack to route each authentication attempt to the
appropriate security level based on the calling service — without fragile
numeric jump counts that break when optional lines are conditionally absent.

### Text Console, GDM & Graphical Login (Strict Mode)

- Standard keys: **PIN required** (`pinverification=1`)
- YubiKey Bio: **fingerprint match** enforced by key firmware
- A stolen key without PIN or enrolled fingerprint cannot unlock your desktop

### Sudo & Terminal Elevation

- Default (`with-pam-u2f` only): **PIN required** (same as graphical login)
- Optional (`with-pam-u2f` + `with-nopin4sudo`): **touch only** (`pinverification=0`) for sudo/polkit/ssh
- YubiKey Bio: touch triggers an internal fingerprint check by the key firmware

---

## How It Works

### Behaviour by service

| Service context      | Standard keys    | YubiKey Bio           |
| :------------------- | :--------------- | :-------------------- |
| GDM / graphical login | Key + PIN        | Key + fingerprint     |
| sudo / polkit / ssh  | Key + PIN (default) or touch-only with `with-nopin4sudo` | Key + fingerprint |

### The paired-guard pattern

The old approach used a single `pam_succeed_if` line with a numeric jump
(e.g. `success=4`) to skip over a block of U2F lines. This is fragile:
authselect's `{include if "..."}` conditionals can remove lines at render
time, making the jump count wrong and causing authentication to overshoot
into the wrong section — which is what prevented the DE from loading.

The new approach wraps each key in a **self-contained guard+key pair**:

```
auth [success=1 default=ignore] pam_succeed_if.so service in <login-svcs> quiet {include if "with-pam-u2f"}
auth sufficient                 pam_u2f.so ... authfile=.ssh/u2f_first         {include if "with-pam-u2f"}
```

- The guard and its key line always carry **identical `{include if}` tags**,
  so they are rendered or stripped as a matched pair — never orphaned.
- The jump is always `success=1`, skipping exactly one line: its partner.
- No jump ever crosses a conditional boundary.

Touch-only blocks use `service in <login-services>` and are enabled only with
`with-nopin4sudo` — the guard fires for GDM/login and skips the key; sudo/etc
fall through to the key normally.

PIN/bio blocks use `service notin <login-services>` and are enabled with
`with-pam-u2f` — the guard fires for sudo/etc and skips the key only when a
touch-only override is active; GDM/login fall through to the key normally.

---

## Key File Convention

Keys are stored as FIDO2 SSH keys in `~/.ssh/` using `sshformat`:

| File               | Key type                  | Notes                              |
| :----------------- | :------------------------ | :--------------------------------- |
| `~/.ssh/u2f_first` | Primary key (PIN capable) | e.g. YubiKey 5C NFC, 5C Nano      |
| `~/.ssh/u2f_second`| Backup key (PIN capable)  | Same type, kept offsite            |
| `~/.ssh/u2f_bio`   | YubiKey Bio               | Fingerprint enforced by firmware   |

For keys without PIN support (older models), a separate file can be added
following the same paired-guard pattern with `pinverification=0`.

---

## Profile Structure

- **`system-auth`**: Primary PAM template. Contains the context-aware U2F
  routing logic for login vs. sudo/polkit/ssh.
- **`password-auth`**: Used for non-interactive password changes and some
  lock screen implementations. Contains PIN-required U2F without routing
  logic (all contexts treated as strict).
- **`nsswitch.conf`**: Name service switch configuration (symlinked from sssd
  base profile unless customised).

